; ======================================================================
;  console.pi4 - THE ONE CONSOLE CORE.
;
;  Anvil's console is ONE thing with several ENDPOINTS, not several
;  parallel consoles. This file is the core: the output hub's terminal
;  MODEL - a character grid with a cursor, wrap and scroll - maintained
;  ONCE, hardware-agnostic. Every endpoint attaches to it:
;
;    * SCREEN endpoints (DMA, V3D, CPU) are RENDERERS of this one grid.
;      They differ only in how they paint it - the DMA renderer blits the
;      cells and DMA-scrolls, the V3D renderer GPU-renders them - and they
;      draw byte-identical content because they read the same grid.
;    * SERIAL / WIFI / ETHERNET endpoints are byte-stream adapters over the
;      same output; they are fed straight off the UART, not from here.
;
;  ONE CONSUMER OF THE OUTPUT STREAM. ConDrainRing() is the ONLY thing
;  that reads the UART mirror ring into the grid. The renderers never
;  consume the ring - they paint whatever the grid says. That is the whole
;  fix for the bugs a second, private V3D grid caused: the boot log is in
;  the grid whichever renderer is active, there is no double-consume, and
;  the scroll happens once. Switch renderers and the SAME grid appears,
;  differing only in sharpness.
;
;  THIS FILE KNOWS NOTHING ABOUT ANY MEDIUM. It has no framebuffer, no
;  V3D, no pixels - just the grid, the cursor, and dirty/scroll bookkeeping
;  the renderers read to paint efficiently. A board that has no display
;  registers no screen renderer and this core still runs (harmlessly) or is
;  simply never pumped; the serial and wireless endpoints are unaffected.
;
;  ======================================================================
;  THE VIEWPORT IS NOT THE CONTENT. 2026-09-10.
;  ======================================================================
;
;  This core used to hold ONE number of rows and use it for two different
;  jobs: how much text is STORED, and how much of it is SHOWN. They were
;  the same number because nothing had ever wanted them to differ, and the
;  only way to show fewer rows was to re-run ConGridInit - which CLEARS
;  THE GRID. Reserving the bottom of the screen for a touch keyboard that
;  way would throw the session's output away to make room for a keyboard,
;  and hiding it again would have nothing to put back.
;
;  So they are two numbers now, and the naming says which is which:
;
;    ConContent*   STORAGE. gConCols x gConRows cells, the scroll, the
;                  text cursor, the history. The keyboard never touches
;                  any of it. This is what survives a show and a hide.
;    ConGrid* / ConView*   THE VIEWPORT. How many rows a renderer paints
;                  and which stored rows they are. Every ConGrid accessor
;                  that takes a ROW INDEX takes a VIEWPORT row, because
;                  that is what a renderer has always meant by it - the
;                  two registered renderers are correct unchanged.
;
;  THE VIEWPORT IS PINNED TO THE CURSOR, at the BOTTOM. ConViewTop() is
;  derived, never stored: the last visible row is the row the next
;  character lands on. That is the property that keeps the prompt and its
;  caret on the glass when the keyboard takes the bottom of the screen -
;  the opposite choice (show the top rows) would reserve space by hiding
;  the one line the operator is typing on.
;
;  AND THE HARDWARE SCROLL HINT IS WITHHELD WHILE THE VIEWPORT IS
;  RESERVED. ConGridScrollN() exists so a renderer can scroll the PICTURE
;  instead of repainting it, and it scrolls the whole console rectangle -
;  whose bottom is now a UI layer this core does not own. Withholding the
;  hint costs a per-row repaint of the visible rows and is the only
;  honest answer; the alternative is a keyboard that walks up the screen.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE TOUCH KEYBOARD'S GEOMETRY, AND WHY THE INCLUDE IS HERE.
;
;  touch_keyboard_view.pbi is pure integer arithmetic - no register, no
;  framebuffer, no medium - so including it does not give this core one.
;  It is included HERE because both of the things that need it are below
;  this file in board.pi4's include order, and the first of them
;  (v3d_console.pi4) is behind #ANVIL_V3D_CONSOLE, so it cannot be the
;  one place the view arrives from. This core calls NOTHING in it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Graphics/touch_keyboard_view.pbi"

#CON_MAXCOLS = 256
#CON_MAXROWS = 80
#CON_CELLS   = 20480              ; 256 * 80

Global Dim gConGrid.a[#CON_CELLS]        ; one byte per cell, ASCII (space = blank)
Global Dim gConRowDirty.a[#CON_MAXROWS]  ; 1 if a row's content changed since paint
Global Dim gConRowBuf.a[#CON_MAXCOLS + 2] ; a renderer's scratch for one NUL-terminated row
Global gConCols.i   = 0
Global gConRows.i   = 0
Global gConCurR.i   = 0                  ; the TEXT cursor (where the next char lands)
Global gConCurC.i   = 0
Global gConScrollN.i = 0                 ; net rows scrolled UP since the last paint,
                                         ; so a renderer can hardware-scroll to match
Global gConFullClear.i = 0               ; the whole console was cleared
Global gConDirty.i  = 0                  ; anything at all changed since the last paint
Global gConReady.i  = 0                  ; geometry has been set

; ---- THE VIEWPORT ----------------------------------------------------
; How many of the gConRows stored rows are currently VISIBLE, and which
; origin the last paint used. gConViewRows = 0 means "not reserved",
; which reads as the whole grid; it is not clamped at assignment so the
; console can be re-sized underneath a reservation without the two
; drifting apart silently.
Global gConViewRows.i    = 0
Global gConViewTopLast.i = 0

; ======================================================================
;  THE UI OVERLAY SEAM.
;
;  A UI layer that is NOT text - today the touch keyboard, tomorrow
;  whatever else - registers a pair of procedures here. The core knows
;  nothing about what they draw; it only knows WHEN they must run:
;
;    paint  after the active screen renderer has painted the grid, and
;           on every forced repaint. The adapter is the thing that knows
;           whether the frame it would be drawing into is owned by a GPU
;           that already composed the layer itself, and it is the only
;           thing that may decide that.
;    reset  when the geometry this core describes stops meaning anything
;           - a new console size, or the screen going away. Whatever the
;           layer had laid out against the old surface is stale, and a
;           stale hit rectangle is a finger landing where nothing is
;           drawn. This fires BEFORE any new geometry is installed.
; ======================================================================
Global *gConOverlay      = 0
Global *gConOverlayReset = 0
Global gConOverlayDirty.i = 0
Global gConOverlayWhole.i = 0

; THE FRAME WAS CLEARED UNDER THE LAYER. A renderer that filled the whole
; console rectangle - a full clear, a viewport change, a forced repaint -
; has erased whatever the layer had on it, so the layer's next pass must
; draw itself whole rather than just the parts it thinks changed. It is a
; latch and not a flag, because the renderer clears its own bookkeeping
; before the layer ever runs.
Procedure.i ConOverlayTakeWhole()
  Define v.i
  v = gConOverlayWhole
  gConOverlayWhole = 0
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

; For a renderer that clears the surface outside this core's own paths -
; a tier handing the screen back, a diagnostic returning it. It says the
; same thing the latch above says and it is a separate entry point only
; so a caller does not have to know the flag's name.
Procedure ConOverlayMarkWhole()
  gConOverlayWhole = 1
EndProcedure

Procedure ConSetOverlay(*paint, *reset)
  *gConOverlay      = *paint
  *gConOverlayReset = *reset
EndProcedure

Procedure ConNoOverlay()
  *gConOverlay      = 0
  *gConOverlayReset = 0
EndProcedure

; The layer changed and nothing in the grid did. Ask for a pass anyway:
; a pressed key has to appear on the next serviced frame whether or not a
; character was printed.
Procedure ConOverlayDamage()
  gConOverlayDirty = 1
  gConDirty = 1
EndProcedure

Procedure ConOverlayReset()
  If *gConOverlayReset <> 0
    gConOverlayReset()
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE VIEWPORT. Derived from the content and the reservation; never a
;  second copy of either.
; ======================================================================
Procedure.i ConContentRows()
  ProcedureReturn gConRows
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ConContentCols()
  ProcedureReturn gConCols
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ConViewRows()
  If gConViewRows < 1 Or gConViewRows > gConRows
    ProcedureReturn gConRows
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn gConViewRows
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ConViewReserved()
  ProcedureReturn Bool(ConViewRows() < gConRows)
EndProcedure

; The first STORED row the viewport shows. Pinned so the text cursor's
; row is the LAST visible one - see the header. Clamped into the content
; at both ends, so a cursor that has not reached the bottom yet shows the
; top of the grid exactly as this console always has.
Procedure.i ConViewTop()
  Define vr.i
  Define t.i
  vr = ConViewRows()
  t = gConCurR - vr + 1
  If t < 0
    t = 0
  EndIf
  If t > (gConRows - vr)
    t = gConRows - vr
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn t
EndProcedure

; Where the text cursor is in VIEWPORT rows - what a caret has to be
; drawn at. -1 if it is not on screen, which cannot happen while the
; viewport is pinned to it but is answered rather than assumed.
Procedure.i ConViewCursorRow()
  Define v.i
  v = gConCurR - ConViewTop()
  If v < 0 Or v >= ConViewRows()
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

Procedure ConGridMarkAll()
  Define r.i
  r = 0
  While r < gConRows
    gConRowDirty[r] = 1
    r = r + 1
  Wend
  gConDirty = 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ConSetViewRows - reserve the bottom of the console for something else.
;
;  IT DOES NOT TOUCH ONE STORED CELL. The grid keeps its size, its
;  history, its scroll position and its cursor; only how much of it is
;  painted changes. That is the whole point of the separation: showing
;  the keyboard must not cost the session's output, and hiding it must
;  put the revealed rows back from what was retained rather than from
;  whatever the screen happened to be showing.
;
;  The full clear is the TRANSITION rectangle and it happens once per
;  show or hide - not per tick, and not per keystroke.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure ConSetViewRows(rows.i)
  Define want.i
  If gConReady = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  want = rows
  If want < 1
    want = 1
  EndIf
  If want > gConRows
    want = gConRows
  EndIf
  If want = ConViewRows()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  gConViewRows = want
  gConFullClear = 1
  gConOverlayWhole = 1
  ConGridMarkAll()
EndProcedure

Procedure ConViewRelease()
  ConSetViewRows(gConRows)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ConMarkViewRowQuiet - this visible row has something on it that is not
;  its text, so repaint it the next time anything is painted. QUIET means
;  it does NOT say the screen changed: saying that would make the next
;  service tick paint, which would mark it again, which would paint
;  again - a console that repaints for ever because something is drawn
;  on it.
;
;  WHY THE OVERLAY NEEDS THIS, AND WHY IT IS EXACTLY RIGHT. A renderer
;  that hardware-scrolls the picture moves everything in the console
;  rectangle up, INCLUDING whatever a UI layer drew there - the keyboard
;  button beside the prompt - and leaves a copy behind, because a row
;  whose text merely moved is not marked dirty. The dirty flags travel
;  with the content in ConGridScroll, so a flag left on the button's rows
;  arrives, after a scroll of any size, on precisely the rows the moved
;  copy landed on. The renderer repaints those from the grid, erasing the
;  copy, and the layer draws itself again on top. No save-under, no
;  special case in the renderer, and the hardware scroll is kept.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure ConMarkViewRowQuiet(v.i)
  Define r.i
  If gConReady = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  r = ConViewTop() + v
  If r < 0 Or r >= gConRows
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  gConRowDirty[r] = 1
EndProcedure

Procedure ConGridClear()
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  n = gConCols * gConRows
  i = 0
  While i < n
    gConGrid[i] = 32
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  gConCurR = 0
  gConCurC = 0
  gConFullClear = 1
  gConOverlayWhole = 1
  gConScrollN = 0
  ConGridMarkAll()
EndProcedure

; ConGridInit - set the grid geometry (columns x rows) and clear it. The
; CALLER works out cols/rows from the display; the model just holds them,
; so it needs nothing board-specific.
Procedure ConGridInit(cols.i, rows.i)
  If cols > #CON_MAXCOLS
    cols = #CON_MAXCOLS
  EndIf
  If rows > #CON_MAXROWS
    rows = #CON_MAXROWS
  EndIf
  If cols < 1
    cols = 1
  EndIf
  If rows < 1
    rows = 1
  EndIf
  ; THE OLD GEOMETRY IS GONE, so anything laid out against it is stale.
  ; This fires BEFORE the new size is installed, so an overlay cannot be
  ; asked to reset against a surface it has never seen.
  ConOverlayReset()
  gConCols = cols
  gConRows = rows
  ; A new console is a whole console: nothing is reserved until something
  ; asks again, which it must do against the size just installed.
  gConViewRows = 0
  gConViewTopLast = 0
  ConGridClear()
  gConReady = 1
EndProcedure

; Scroll the whole grid up one row. The dirty flags shift with the content
; so a renderer that hardware-scrolls the picture only has to repaint the
; rows that genuinely changed, exactly like the DMA console always did.
Procedure ConGridScroll()
  Define r.i
  Define c.i
  r = 0
  While r < gConRows - 1
    c = 0
    While c < gConCols
      gConGrid[r * gConCols + c] = gConGrid[(r + 1) * gConCols + c]
      c = c + 1
    Wend
    gConRowDirty[r] = gConRowDirty[r + 1]
    r = r + 1
  Wend
  c = 0
  While c < gConCols
    gConGrid[(gConRows - 1) * gConCols + c] = 32
    c = c + 1
  Wend
  gConRowDirty[gConRows - 1] = 1
  gConScrollN = gConScrollN + 1
  gConDirty = 1
  ; WITH THE BOTTOM RESERVED THERE IS NO HARDWARE SCROLL TO MATCH - see
  ; the header. Every visible row's source moved, so every visible row is
  ; repainted instead; the rows the keyboard covers are not among them.
  If ConViewReserved() <> 0
    ConGridMarkAll()
  EndIf
EndProcedure

Procedure ConGridNewline()
  gConCurC = 0
  gConCurR = gConCurR + 1
  If gConCurR >= gConRows
    ConGridScroll()
    gConCurR = gConRows - 1
  EndIf
  ; The viewport is pinned to the cursor, so moving the cursor can move
  ; the window over the content with nothing in the content changing.
  ; Say the screen is dirty, or that move never reaches the glass.
  If ConViewReserved() <> 0
    gConDirty = 1
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; The terminal state machine, one byte at a time. Newline, carriage
; return, backspace, tab, wrap and scroll - the ordinary console rules,
; kept ONCE here for every renderer.
Procedure ConGridPutc(c.i)
  Define t.i
  If gConReady = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If c = 13                          ; CR
    gConCurC = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If c = 10                          ; LF
    ConGridNewline()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If c = 8 Or c = 127                ; backspace / delete
    If gConCurC > 0
      gConCurC = gConCurC - 1
      gConGrid[gConCurR * gConCols + gConCurC] = 32
      gConRowDirty[gConCurR] = 1
      gConDirty = 1
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If c = 9                           ; tab -> next multiple of 8
    t = (gConCurC + 8) - ((gConCurC + 8) % 8)
    While gConCurC < t And gConCurC < gConCols
      gConGrid[gConCurR * gConCols + gConCurC] = 32
      gConCurC = gConCurC + 1
    Wend
    gConRowDirty[gConCurR] = 1
    gConDirty = 1
    If gConCurC >= gConCols
      ConGridNewline()
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If c < 32 Or c > 126               ; other control bytes: ignore
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  gConGrid[gConCurR * gConCols + gConCurC] = c
  gConRowDirty[gConCurR] = 1
  gConDirty = 1
  gConCurC = gConCurC + 1
  If gConCurC >= gConCols
    ConGridNewline()
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ConDrainRing - THE ONE CONSUMER of the UART mirror ring. Drains it into
; the grid. ScreenPump calls it before painting, and uart.pi4 may call it as
; a MODEL-ONLY producer-side drain at a line boundary / high-water mark. It
; never renders and never prints, so that callback is safe inside UartWrite.
Procedure ConDrainRing()
  Define n.i
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  If gConReady = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  n = UartMirrorCount()
  i = 0
  While i < n
    c = UartMirrorGet()
    If c < 0
      Break
    EndIf
    ConGridPutc(c)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

; ---- read-only accessors ---------------------------------------------
;
;  ConContent*  STORAGE, indexed by stored row. What survives a show and
;               a hide of the keyboard.
;  ConGrid*     THE VIEWPORT, indexed by VISIBLE row, which is what a
;               renderer has always meant by a row index. With nothing
;               reserved the two are identical and every existing caller
;               is correct unchanged.
Procedure.i ConGridReady()
  ProcedureReturn gConReady
EndProcedure
Procedure.i ConGridCols()
  ProcedureReturn gConCols
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ConContentCell(r.i, c.i)
  If r < 0 Or r >= gConRows Or c < 0 Or c >= gConCols
    ProcedureReturn 32
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn gConGrid[r * gConCols + c] & $FF
EndProcedure
Procedure.i ConContentRowDirty(r.i)
  If r < 0 Or r >= gConRows
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn gConRowDirty[r]
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ConGridRows()
  ProcedureReturn ConViewRows()
EndProcedure
Procedure.i ConGridCell(r.i, c.i)
  ProcedureReturn ConContentCell(ConViewTop() + r, c)
EndProcedure
Procedure.i ConGridRowDirty(r.i)
  If r < 0 Or r >= ConViewRows()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; THE WINDOW MOVED, SO EVERY VISIBLE ROW IS SHOWING SOMETHING ELSE.
  ; The stored row's own dirty flag says nothing about that: the content
  ; is untouched and the picture is entirely wrong.
  If gConViewTopLast <> ConViewTop()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn ConContentRowDirty(ConViewTop() + r)
EndProcedure
Procedure.i ConGridScrollN()
  If ConViewReserved() <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn gConScrollN
EndProcedure
Procedure.i ConGridFullClear()
  ProcedureReturn gConFullClear
EndProcedure
Procedure.i ConGridDirty()
  ProcedureReturn gConDirty
EndProcedure
Procedure.i ConGridCurR()
  ProcedureReturn gConCurR
EndProcedure
Procedure.i ConGridCurC()
  ProcedureReturn gConCurC
EndProcedure

; Build a NUL-terminated string of one grid row into a caller's buffer,
; trimmed of trailing spaces. Returns the length (0 for a blank row). The
; renderers use this to paint a row and to know how much of it is ink.
Procedure.i ConContentRowText(r.i, *dst)
  Define c.i
  Define last.i
  Define g.i
  If r < 0 Or r >= gConRows
    PokeA(*dst, 0)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  last = -1
  c = 0
  While c < gConCols
    g = gConGrid[r * gConCols + c] & $FF
    If g = 0
      g = 32
    EndIf
    PokeA(*dst + c, g)
    If g <> 32
      last = c
    EndIf
    c = c + 1
  Wend
  PokeA(*dst + last + 1, 0)
  ProcedureReturn last + 1
EndProcedure

; The same row, addressed the way a renderer addresses it.
Procedure.i ConGridRowText(r.i, *dst)
  ProcedureReturn ConContentRowText(ConViewTop() + r, *dst)
EndProcedure

; Called by a renderer once it has painted the grid, to clear the change
; bookkeeping. The NEXT change re-sets it. The viewport origin is recorded
; here and nowhere else, so "the window moved" is answered against the
; origin the picture on the glass was actually drawn at.
Procedure ConGridClearDirty()
  Define r.i
  r = 0
  While r < gConRows
    gConRowDirty[r] = 0
    r = r + 1
  Wend
  gConScrollN = 0
  gConFullClear = 0
  gConDirty = 0
  gConViewTopLast = ConViewTop()
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE SCREEN ENDPOINT REGISTRY + THE PUMP.
;
;  A SCREEN renderer is an endpoint that paints the grid. Exactly one is
;  active at a time (the current display tier); it registers itself with
;  ConSetRenderer(@its_paint_proc). The core calls it through the pointer,
;  so it knows nothing about V3D, DMA or CPU - a new renderer type is added
;  by writing a paint proc and registering it, touching nothing here. With
;  no display, no renderer is registered and the pump paints nothing; the
;  serial and wireless endpoints, fed straight off the UART, are unaffected.
; ======================================================================
Global *gConPaint = 0            ; the active screen renderer's paint proc

Procedure ConSetRenderer(*fn)
  *gConPaint = *fn
EndProcedure
Procedure ConNoRenderer()
  *gConPaint = 0
EndProcedure

; Take the grid down (the screen went away). Output keeps flowing to the
; serial and wireless endpoints; there is just nothing painting the grid.
;
; The overlay is RESET but stays registered: the surface went away, not
; the feature. Its geometry is invalidated so nothing can be hit-tested
; against rectangles that describe a screen this monitor is no longer
; drawing on, and it lays itself out again when a screen comes back.
Procedure ConGridStop()
  ConOverlayReset()
  gConReady = 0
  gConViewRows = 0
  gConViewTopLast = 0
  gConOverlayDirty = 0
  *gConPaint = 0
EndProcedure

; ScreenPump - THE HUB. Drain the one output stream into the one grid, then
; let the active screen renderer paint what changed. This is the single
; entry every caller (the prompt, the boot log, the payload boundary) uses;
; the name is kept because four files already call it.
Procedure ScreenPump()
  If gConReady = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  ConDrainRing()
  If gConDirty = 0 And gConOverlayDirty = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If *gConPaint <> 0
    gConPaint()
    ; THE UI LAYER LAST, AND BELOW THE POINTER. The renderer has finished
    ; with the frame; whether this pass may touch it at all is the
    ; adapter's decision, because only the adapter knows whether a GPU
    ; composed the layer inside the frame it just presented.
    ;
    ; NO RENDERER MEANS NO UI LAYER. A diagnostic that takes the screen
    ; unregisters the renderer (ConNoRenderer) and paints the whole
    ; surface itself; drawing an overlay on top of it would be this core
    ; scribbling on something it does not own. The layout is kept, not
    ; thrown away, so the restore is a repaint and not a re-layout.
    If *gConOverlay <> 0
      gConOverlay()
    EndIf
  EndIf
  gConOverlayDirty = 0
EndProcedure

; ConRepaint - force the whole grid to be repainted by the current
; renderer (used the instant a new tier takes over, so the freshly
; registered renderer shows the existing grid rather than a blank screen).
Procedure ConRepaint()
  If gConReady = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  ConGridMarkAll()
  gConFullClear = 1
  gConOverlayWhole = 1
  If *gConPaint <> 0
    gConPaint()
    If *gConOverlay <> 0
      gConOverlay()
    EndIf
  EndIf
  gConOverlayDirty = 0
EndProcedure
